<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles I of Anjou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1226">1226</a>–<a href="/years/1285">1285</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Charles of Anjou · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Capetian House of Anjou</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles I of Anjou was the youngest brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, Count of Anjou and of Provence through his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatrice-of-provence">Beatrice of Provence</a> in 1246. Invited by the pope, he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> and took the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1266. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a> of 1282 lost him the island to Aragon, leaving his heirs the mainland Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou.jpg" alt="Charles I of Anjou" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Son of Groucho from Scotland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Some_Old_Guy_2_(5642308121)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was the youngest child of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-viii-of-france">Louis VIII of France</a> and Blanche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and the first Capetian to be named for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. The late king willed that his youngest sons be prepared for a career in the Church, and Charles was destined for it until the early 1240s. He received a good education and had a documented passion for poetry, medicine and law. In December 1244 Louis IX took a vow to lead a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and Charles took the cross too, despite his mother's opposition. The crusaders took Damietta in 1249, and the brothers were captured on 6 April 1250 and released for a ransom. Charles returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in October 1250.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Charles held Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forcalquier">Forcalquier</a> through his wife's inheritance. After returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> he put down a rebellion in Provence: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> surrendered in April 1251, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> in May, and Marseille, the last to resist, made peace in July 1252. In 1263 he accepted the Holy See's offer of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, called the Regno. He was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 5 January 1266, annihilated Manfred's army and occupied the Regno almost without resistance. His victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a> at Tagliacozzo in 1268 secured his rule. In 1272 he took the title King of Albania, in 1277 he purchased a claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, and in 1278 he became Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>.</p><p>His rule in the Regno rested on heavy taxation, notably the subventio generalis, which the popes called illegal. In 1281 Pope Martin IV authorised a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a> rebellion of 30 March 1282 ended his rule on the island, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a> was declared king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on 4 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles prepared a campaign to regain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, but fell seriously ill, made his will on 6 January 1285 and died on 7 January. His body was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and his heart was placed at the Couvent Saint-Jacques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Two realms, each ruled by a king styled of Sicily, then coexisted for more than a century. The chroniclers Bartholomaeus of Neocastro and Saba Malaspina strongly influenced modern views of Charles, though both were biased: the first called him a tyrant to justify the Vespers. He gave estates in the kingdom to about 700 noblemen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or Provence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles I of Anjou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…dynasty’s greatest test came in 1270, when Louis IX of France landed at Carthage on what became the Eighth Crusade; disease ravaged the camp, Louis died near Tunis in August, and <b>Charles of Anjou</b> negotiated a treaty in which al-Mustansir paid an indemnity and renewed tribute to Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles of Anjou</b>, brother of Louis IX of France, acquired the County of Provence by marrying its heiress Beatrice in 1246, bringing it into the orbit of the French royal family and later of the Angevin kings of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1265 Pope Clement IV invested <b>Charles of Anjou</b>, brother of Louis IX of France, with the kingdom; in February 1266 Charles defeated and killed King Manfred at the Battle of Benevento and in 1268 defeated the last Hohenstaufen heir, Conradin, at the Battle of Tagliacozzo, beheading him…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Manfred, Frederick’s son, was killed at the Battle of Benevento in 1266 by <b>Charles of Anjou</b>, who then defeated Conradin at Tagliacozzo in 1268.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After William II accepted the suzerainty of <b>Charles of Anjou</b> in the Treaty of Viterbo in 1267, the principality passed on his death in 1278 to the Angevin kings of Naples, who governed through bailiffs and granted it to members of their family or to the husbands of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Using a hidden reserve, <b>Charles I of Anjou</b> defeated Conradin, the sixteen-year-old last heir of the Hohenstaufen, who had come to reclaim the Kingdom of Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-23">23 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Foggia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles I of Anjou"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
